NList.mcc/NList.mcc

    *******************************************************************
    Copyright (C) 2001-2014 NList Open Source Team
    $Id$

    This MCC public custom class is very similar to the MUI's list
    class.

    It handles directly most attributes which are handled by Listview
    in the original couple List/Listview.

    The NListview exist anyway to provide a complete object with
    scrollbars, so you should use it as child of NListview. Anyway,
    you can use NList without NListview if you don't want any builtin
    scrollbar.

    NOTE: NList class will not work with Listview without some
          conflicts, and NListview class can't use List as child but
          only NList or a NList subclass.

          MUIM_NList_TestPos and MUIM_List_TestPos are similar
          but use a different struct to store data.
          MUIM_List_TestPos works like with a List object.

    NOTE: Avoid as possible to do many things in MUIM_Show and
          MUIM_Hide methods because when an object is in
          a virtual group, your object will receive them
          for each one of its moves !!!


    Standard tags with NList special values :

        MUIA_Background   has the same meaning than
                          MUIA_NList_ListBackground
                          but only at init.

        MUIA_Font         is settable only at init.
                          MUIV_NList_Font, MUIV_NList_Font_Little
                          and MUIV_NList_Font_Fixed are special
                          values usable for it (settable in prefs)
                          but standard values are usable too.

        MUIA_Frame        you can override the defaults frames
                          of the classe by setting it, but it will
                          be overrided again by defaults if a
                          set(obj,MUIA_NList_Input,bool) is made after.
                          if MUIA_NList_Input is TRUE then the
                          default is MUIV_Frame_InputList, if FALSE
                          it's MUIV_Frame_ReadList.

        MUIA_ContextMenu
        MUIM_ContextMenuBuild
        MUIM_ContextMenuChoice
                          read MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild.


    The gadget was originally written in 1996 by Gilles Masson. In 2001
    it was released under the LGPL license and is now maintained by a
    free Open Source Team.

    It is released and distributed under the terms of the GNU Lesser
    General Public License (LGPL) and available free of charge.

    Please visit http://www.sf.net/projects/nlist-classes/ for
    the very latest version and information regarding NList
    *******************************************************************

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Active

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Active -- Read or change the active display row.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get/notify; LONG, default MUIV_NList_Active_Off (-1).
        List_Active is a compatibility alias with the MUIA_ prefix.

    VALUES
        Zero-based display index, or MUIV_NList_Active_Off (-1), Top (-2),
        Bottom (-3), Up (-4), Down (-5), PageUp (-6), PageDown (-7).
        Abbreviated names use the MUIV_NList_Active_ prefix.

    FUNCTION
        In ordinary input-enabled line mode, set the active row and bring it
        into view. Top/Bottom select the first/last row; Up/Down move one
        row. PageUp/PageDown first move to the visible edge and then move by
        Visible minus one. Invalid numeric positions are ignored. Off clears
        the active position; in single-selection mode it also deselects the
        previous active row.

        Active and selected are distinct in multiselect mode. Selection
        changes follow the current list-selection preference and
        MultiTestHook; activation does not prove that the row became
        selected. Repeating the same active value normally does nothing.

        In character mode or with Input FALSE, the implementation uses a
        separate navigation position and does not establish a normal active
        row. Some selectors differ: the attribute's Bottom path derives from
        that stored position instead of the final row. Do not use these
        modes to obtain ordinary activation semantics. Get reads the actual
        Active field.

        Constructor Active is applied only within the SourceInsert,
        SourceString or SourceArray initialization branch. A later set
        rewrites the tag to a resolved value or TAG_IGNORE; supply writable
        tags.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_SetActive, MUIM_NList_Jump, MUIA_NList_TypeSelect

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_ActiveObjectOnClick

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_ActiveObjectOnClick -- [ISG], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        If set to TRUE, the NList object will become the
        MUIA_Window_ActiveObject of its window when you
        click on it. In addition, the active selected entries
        will switch an inactive color (e.g. grey) in case the
        NList object isn't currently active. As soon as the
        object is active it will then also receive all user
        keys pressed.

    DEFAULT
        FALSE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DefaultObjectOnClick
        MUIA_NList_KeepActive
        MUIA_NList_MakeActive
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_AdjustHeight

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_AdjustHeight -- [I..], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIA_List_AdjustHeight.

        Will adjust the nlist height if the nlist object is in a virtual
        group or if MUIA_NList_SourceInsert, MUIA_NList_SourceString,
        MUIA_NList_SourceArray or MUIA_List_SourceArray was used.

        When the object is in a virtual group, a re-layout of this one
        will be forced when the entries number of the NList object change,
        so all entries should always be visible.

    DEFAULT
        FALSE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_List_AdjustHeight, MUIA_NList_AdjustWidth
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_AdjustWidth

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_AdjustWidth -- [I..], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIA_List_AdjustWidth.

        Will adjust the nlist width if the nlist object is in a virtual
        group or if MUIA_NList_SourceInsert, MUIA_NList_SourceString,
        MUIA_NList_SourceArray or MUIA_List_SourceArray was used.

    DEFAULT
        FALSE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_List_AdjustWidth, MUIA_NList_AdjustHeight

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_AutoClip

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_AutoClip -- [ISG], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        If set to TRUE and the NList object is in read-only (NoInput) mode and
        is set to select char-wide rather than by line, the selected content is
        immediately copied to the clipboard as soon as it is selected via the
        mouse.

    DEFAULT
        TRUE

    WARNING
        Please note that due to historical reasons this feature is turned
        on per default. However, due to violating the Amiga style guides, new
        or revised applications are adviced to disable auto clipping as
        manually performing the copy operation should be the preferred method.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Input
        MUIA_NList_TypeSelect
        MUIA_NList_AutoCopyToClip
        MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook
        MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook
        MUIM_NList_CopyToClip
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_AutoCopyToClip

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_AutoCopyToClip -- [IS.], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        If set to TRUE you can copy the selected area to the
        clipboard 0 with Amiga-C and Amiga-X (Amiga-X because
        i have seen that sometimes Amiga-C is a shortcut !).
        (works with both Right-Amiga and Left-Amiga keys)

        MUIA_NList_AutoCopyToClip == TRUE also requires that the NList object
        is either the window's active object (see MUIA_Window_ActiveObject) or
        the attached list object is window's default object (see
        MUIA_Window_DefaultObject). Otherwise nothing will ever be copied!

    DEFAULT
        TRUE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_AutoClip
        MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook
        MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook
        MUIM_NList_CopyToClip
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_AutoVisible

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_AutoVisible -- Keep a valid active row visible during
        list updates.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get; BOOL, default FALSE. MUIA_List_AutoVisible is a
        compatibility alias.

    FUNCTION
        When an operation invokes the class active-visibility helper, TRUE
        moves First only as needed to include a valid Active row. It does
        not center the row. Setting this flag merely stores it; the setter
        does not itself jump or redraw.

        Active and SetActive also have their own jump handling. FALSE does
        not disable every scroll caused by activation. This is not a promise
        to continuously enforce visibility after every external scroll.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Active, MUIM_NList_Jump, MUIM_NList_SetActive

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_ButtonClick

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_ButtonClick -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        You'll get a notify on it each time the user
        clicks one of the buttons made by ESC O[...@<n>] or ESC o[...@<n>].

        The returned value is the <n> of the clicked image/object.
        (see MUIA_NList_DisplayHook for more).

        If you get that value later, it will still be <n> of the latest
        image/object clicked that you'll get.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook, MUIA_NList_DoubleClick, MUIA_NList_MultiClick,
        MUIA_NList_EntryClick, MUIA_NList_TitleClick
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_ClickColumn

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_ClickColumn -- [..G], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Same function as Listview.mui/MUIA_Listview_ClickColumn.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_Listview_ClickColumn, MUIA_NList_DefClickColumn
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Columns

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Columns -- Read or set the visible column order.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get/notify; BYTE * containing display-array indices
        in visible order, terminated by signed -1. NULL leaves the order
        unchanged.

    FUNCTION
        Supply a valid signed-byte array ending in -1 or containing at least
        one element for every current column. NList reads no more than its
        current column count. Each index identifies an existing COL from
        Format; applying it swaps that descriptor into the corresponding
        visible position. A shorter array changes only its prefix, but swaps
        can move other columns. Invalid or absent indices do nothing; values
        below -1 do not hide columns.

        Get returns an object-owned array with a trailing -1. Do not free it
        or retain it beyond the object lifetime. Its contents are refreshed
        by later column queries or notifications. You may edit this array
        and set it back, or supply your own array; setting copies the order
        into descriptor associations and retains no input pointer.

        User reordering requests Columns notification. Programmatic
        setter/method paths clear the pending Columns bit after applying the
        order; do not rely on receiving a notification for every
        programmatic assignment. Query the resulting order when confirmation
        is required.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol, MUIM_NList_ColToColumn,
        MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_CompareHook

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_CompareHook -- Select entry comparison.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; struct Hook *, default NULL. The legacy form uses
        the classic hook object/message arguments.

    FUNCTION
        Called through MUIM_NList_Compare with entry2 as hook object and
        entry1 as hook message (A2/A1 in the classic convention). Return a
        signed value less than, equal to or greater than zero for entry1
        before, equal to or after entry2. Do not assume the result magnitude
        is exactly one.

        The default NULL hook uses Stricmp, so payloads must be readable
        NUL-terminated strings unless comparison is overridden. This legacy
        hook does not receive sort types as arguments. Use CompareHook2 or
        override Compare to interpret them. Changing the comparison hook
        does not automatically sort the list.

        The hook pointer is borrowed. Keep the hook and its code valid while
        installed. No getter is implemented locally. List_CompareHook is a
        legacy alias (with the MUIA_ prefix). At construction, the NList
        alias takes precedence over the List alias, and Hook2 overrides
        either legacy form. During set, the last processed form selects the
        calling convention.

        Changing the hook does not reconstruct or dispose existing entries.
        Keep construction and destruction policies compatible with every
        retained payload. The legacy hook helper temporarily supplies the
        list object in h_Data when h_Data was NULL; otherwise it preserves
        the existing value.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_CompareHook2, MUIM_NList_Compare

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_CompareHook2

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_CompareHook2 -- Select entry comparison. Use the message
        hook convention.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; borrowed struct Hook * or NULL. No local getter.

    FUNCTION
        Use the same payload and result rules as MUIA_NList_CompareHook, but
        the hook object is the NList object and the hook message describes
        entry1, entry2, sort_type and sort_type2 in NList_CompareMessage.
        The message is temporary; do not retain its address. Use the
        platform header and hook convention.

        At construction this form overrides the legacy form. During set the
        last processed form wins. Unlike the legacy helper, this path does
        not supply a missing h_Data value. Changing the form does not
        transform existing payloads. The header identifies sort_type2 as an
        addition in version 19.96; do not assume that field exists in older
        implementations.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_CompareHook, MUIM_NList_Compare

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_ConstructHook

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_ConstructHook -- Select entry construction.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; struct Hook *, default NULL. The legacy form uses
        the classic hook object/message arguments.

    FUNCTION
        Called through MUIM_NList_Construct with pool as hook object and the
        input entry as hook message (A2/A1 in the classic convention).
        Return the retained entry pointer, or NULL to reject it. The default
        NULL hook retains the original pointer without copying. Keep
        borrowed entry data valid until removal, replacement, clear or
        disposal.

        MUIV_NList_ConstructHook_String (-1) selects the built-in copier. It
        copies only up to the first NUL, LF or CR, adds a NUL terminator and
        allocates from the supplied pool. Pair it with
        MUIV_NList_DestructHook_String on DestructHook. Allocation failure
        returns NULL.

        The hook pointer is borrowed. Keep the hook and its code valid while
        installed. No getter is implemented locally. List_ConstructHook is a
        legacy alias (with the MUIA_ prefix). At construction, the NList
        alias takes precedence over the List alias, and Hook2 overrides
        either legacy form. During set, the last processed form selects the
        calling convention.

        Changing the hook does not reconstruct or dispose existing entries.
        Keep construction and destruction policies compatible with every
        retained payload. The legacy hook helper temporarily supplies the
        list object in h_Data when h_Data was NULL; otherwise it preserves
        the existing value.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_ConstructHook2, MUIM_NList_Construct

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_ConstructHook2

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_ConstructHook2 -- Select entry construction. Use the
        message hook convention.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; borrowed struct Hook * or NULL. No local getter.

    FUNCTION
        Use the same payload and result rules as MUIA_NList_ConstructHook,
        but the hook object is the NList object and the hook message
        describes entry and pool in NList_ConstructMessage. The message is
        temporary; do not retain its address. Use the platform header and
        hook convention.

        At construction this form overrides the legacy form. During set the
        last processed form wins. Unlike the legacy helper, this path does
        not supply a missing h_Data value. Changing the form does not
        transform existing payloads. The String sentinel is recognized only
        by the legacy attribute, not by Hook2.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_ConstructHook, MUIM_NList_Construct

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook -- [IS.], struct Hook *

    FUNCTION
        This hook will be called while a MUIM_NList_CopyToClip
        for each column string. You'll certainly get in entry
        a string given by your own MUIA_NList_DisplayHook/2, so
        you must not use the same buffer for both !

        You'll get the entry num in -1 element of the given array,
        elements 1 and 2 are the positions of the first selected
        char and the last+1.

        You must return the pointer of the string to copy to
        clipboard in element 0, a the string length in element 1.

        The builtin hook skip all ESC chars plus their next char
        (and [...] for ESC-P, ESC-I, ESC-O and ESC-o), and add a
        tab char between columns.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook,
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook2

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook2 -- [IS.], struct Hook *

    FUNCTION
        Same function as MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook but A2 will be the object
        and A1 a NList_CopyColumnToClipMessage struct pointer.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook -- [IS.], struct Hook *

    FUNCTION
        This work near like MUIA_NList_DisplayHook, execpt
        that it is not called when the NList object want to
        display its lines but when it want to copy them
        to clipboard (or other). See MUIM_NList_CopyToClip.

        You can return only one string pointer (only one
        column for copy), as element 0 of the array.

        The -1 element is the entry number only when
        you don't give a entry pointer to NList_CopyToClip method,
        else it's -1.

        Elements 1,2,3 and 4 of the given array are first column/pos
        and last column/pos which are selected.
        Elements 5 and 6 are 2, 1 or 0 when the 1th and 3rd pos are
        in the format preparse string, the special entry preparse string
        or in the normal string for that entry/columns.

        For column, -1 is the first and -2 the last, else it's its number.
        This is the number of displayed columns and not the corresponding
        entry in the array return by DisplayHook. Anyway, positions are
        calculated from strings returned by DisplayHook.

        For pos, -1 is left of column and -2 its end. The last pos should
        not be included.

        You should use MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook unless you don't
        want to be copied what is seen in the list.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, MUIA_NList_CopyColumnToClipHook,
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook2

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook2 -- [IS.], struct Hook *

    FUNCTION
        Same function as MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook but A2 will be the object
        and A1 a NList_CopyEntryToClipMessage struct pointer.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_CopyEntryToClipHook
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DefaultObjectOnClick

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DefaultObjectOnClick -- [ISG], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        If set to TRUE, the NList object will become the
        MUIA_Window_DefaultObject of its window when you
        click on it, so the user will be able to control
        the list with keys. The MUIA_Window_ActiveObject
        will be set to None, unless the current active
        object is the NList one itself or if it's the
        MUIA_NList_KeepActive one.

        There is a special meaning if you use both
        DefaultObjectOnClick and MUIA_NList_MakeActive.
        (see MUIA_NList_MakeActive)

    DEFAULT
        TRUE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_KeepActive,MUIA_NList_MakeActive
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DefClickColumn

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DefClickColumn -- Choose the column used for keyboard
        activation coordinates.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get; LONG visible-column position, default 0.
        MUIA_Listview_DefClickColumn is a compatibility alias.

    FUNCTION
        For MUIKEY_PRESS with Input enabled, line-selection mode and a valid
        Active row, NList synthesizes click coordinates using this visible
        column. Values outside the current range fall back to column zero.
        The horizontal coordinate is clipped to the viewport; the reported
        hit need not remain in an offscreen requested column.

        This is a visible position, not a display-array COL index. Keyboard
        activation requests DoubleClick/Listview DoubleClick and, when
        neither is wanted, EntryClick. It does not simulate a title-button
        click. Setting the attribute stores the value without validation or
        activation.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_ClickColumn, MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DestructHook

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DestructHook -- Select entry destruction.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; struct Hook *, default NULL. The legacy form uses
        the classic hook object/message arguments.

    FUNCTION
        Called through MUIM_NList_Destruct with pool as hook object and the
        constructed payload as hook message (A2/A1 in the classic
        convention). Release only storage owned by this policy, using the
        matching allocator. The hook result is ignored. The default NULL
        hook does not free payloads.

        MUIV_NList_DestructHook_String (-1) selects the built-in
        FreeVecPooled destructor for the matching string constructor. It is
        not suitable for arbitrary borrowed pointers. Logical entries are
        destructed once; wrapped continuation rows do not own separate
        payloads.

        The hook pointer is borrowed. Keep the hook and its code valid while
        installed. No getter is implemented locally. List_DestructHook is a
        legacy alias (with the MUIA_ prefix). At construction, the NList
        alias takes precedence over the List alias, and Hook2 overrides
        either legacy form. During set, the last processed form selects the
        calling convention.

        Changing the hook does not reconstruct or dispose existing entries.
        Keep construction and destruction policies compatible with every
        retained payload. The legacy hook helper temporarily supplies the
        list object in h_Data when h_Data was NULL; otherwise it preserves
        the existing value.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DestructHook2, MUIM_NList_Destruct

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DestructHook2

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DestructHook2 -- Select entry destruction. Use the
        message hook convention.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; borrowed struct Hook * or NULL. No local getter.

    FUNCTION
        Use the same payload and result rules as MUIA_NList_DestructHook,
        but the hook object is the NList object and the hook message
        describes entry and pool in NList_DestructMessage. The message is
        temporary; do not retain its address. Use the platform header and
        hook convention.

        At construction this form overrides the legacy form. During set the
        last processed form wins. Unlike the legacy helper, this path does
        not supply a missing h_Data value. Changing the form does not
        transform existing payloads. The String sentinel is recognized only
        by the legacy attribute, not by Hook2.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DestructHook, MUIM_NList_Destruct

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DisplayHook

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook -- Supply column text through a legacy
        display hook.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; borrowed struct Hook *, default NULL.
        MUIA_List_DisplayHook is a compatibility alias. No class-specific
        getter.

    FUNCTION
        The hook receives the string array as its object argument and the
        stored entry payload as its message argument. These correspond to A2
        and A1 in the classic hook convention; use the target AROS hook ABI.
        For a title, the payload is NULL. If h_Data is NULL, NList
        temporarily sets it to the NList object during this legacy callback,
        then restores NULL.

        The string array has DISPLAY_ARRAY_MAX (64) text slots followed by
        64 preparse slots. Fill strings[col] and, optionally,
        strings[64+col] for each COL used by Format, even after visible
        reordering. The used slots start as NULL and slot zero initially
        contains the payload or title pointer. With no hook or subclass
        override, that pointer is interpreted as the first column's text.

        The implementation stores position in the pointer-sized slot
        strings[-1], and PrivateData in strings[-2] for the legacy call.
        Position is a zero-based owner row or -1 for the title. Do not read
        an assumed 32-bit word immediately before strings on every target.
        Writing the position slot to pointer-sized -2 prevents reuse on the
        next lookup. Prefer DisplayRecall when invalidating from outside the
        callback.

        Text and preparses remain owned by the application. Keep them valid
        and unchanged while NList can reuse them, including after the
        callback returns. NList caches pointers and calls the hook for
        measurement as well as drawing; it does not promise one call per
        repaint. Return consistent text for the same state. If buffers
        change outside the callback, set DisplayRecall before the next use
        and request Redraw when geometry or pixels must be refreshed.

        Installing a hook invalidates the display cache and schedules
        column, scrollbar and wrapping work. At construction, DisplayHook2
        takes precedence if both forms are supplied. Later setters share one
        hook pointer; the last processed form selects the calling
        convention. The callback result is ignored by the internal display-
        array caller.

    TEXT FORMAT
        ESC denotes byte 27, written "\033" in a C string. Text processing
        normally stops at NUL, LF or CR. The initial ordinary-text parsing
        window is 300 bytes; each preparse window is 100 bytes. Wrapped
        continuations use their recorded slice and skip preparses. Escape-
        argument scans can extend beyond those windows; they are not a
        general input-length guarantee.

        ESC u, b and i enable underline, bold and italic; ESC n restores
        normal style. ESC l, c, r and j select left, centered, right and
        justified alignment. Put alignment codes at the beginning of the
        text or preparse. ESC - disables subsequent escape interpretation;
        tab and fixed-space processing still applies. Unknown escape
        commands are consumed without producing their command character.

        Tab advances to a tab boundary (TabSize defaults to 8). Byte 0xA0 is
        a fixed-width space unless IgnoreSpecialChars disables its special
        treatment. ESC 2 through ESC 9 select the corresponding DrawInfo pen
        while shown. ESC P[n] selects a direct pen number; ESC P[] restores
        the supplied default pen. The caller owns any pen allocation
        required for direct pen numbers.

        ESC T, C, B and E request horizontal lines at the top, center,
        bottom, or beside the text. ESC t selects thick-line styling with
        the fill pen. ESC t[n], t[Mn] and t[In] select a direct pen, MUI pen
        0..8, or DrawInfo pen 0..11; DrawInfo indices 9..11 map to index 2.
        Prefix N inside the brackets for a thin line; t[N] uses the shadow
        pen, not an unconditional black pen.

        ESC I[spec] passes the bracket contents as an image specification.
        This path does not parse the size or button suffixes described
        below. ESC O[p] uses a valid CreateImage handle represented in
        hexadecimal; ESC o[n] uses a decimal UseImage index. Keep referenced
        images valid. Do not assume an eight-digit pointer representation on
        every AROS target.

        O/o suffixes are parsed in order: optional @button, optional
        ;tag;value in hexadecimal, optional ,minimum, optional |width or
        |width|height, and optional ,minimum after the size. Width, height,
        minimum and button are decimal. Minimum reserves horizontal space. A
        repeated minimum after the size replaces the earlier one. The tag-
        field scanner does not stop at a vertical bar, so separate tag
        fields from size fields with a comma/minimum field; directly
        appended size fields can be skipped.

        For object-backed images, width <= 0 uses the natural width and
        negative height uses natural height; height 0 uses the row height.
        With a minimum and default width -1, the minimum can enlarge width
        and select row height unless height -1 was explicit. For bitmap-
        backed images, width is the bitmap width regardless of the requested
        width; height is limited to bitmap height except that 0 requests row
        height. Button/tag metadata applies to the object-backed path;
        bitmap records discard it. An unresolved o index draws no bitmap,
        but a positive minimum can still reserve space. Use only valid image
        handles and well-formed escape sequences.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook2, MUIM_NList_Display,
        MUIA_NList_DisplayRecall, MUIA_NList_Format

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DisplayHook2

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook2 -- Supply column text with the NList object
        as hook context.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; borrowed struct Hook *, default NULL. No class-
        specific getter.

    FUNCTION
        Uses the same text, lifetime, caching and formatting contract as
        DisplayHook. The object argument is the NList object; the message is
        the internal display-message storage corresponding to
        NList_DisplayMessage: entry, entry_pos, strings[64] and
        preparses[64]. Entry is NULL and position -1 for a title. This form
        does not temporarily replace h_Data.

        NList passes its internal pointer array directly, not a separately
        populated structure. Use the target AROS header/ABI; do not
        substitute a host-defined packed structure. The internal layout and
        calling convention must match the target. To override display
        through a typed method message instead, implement
        MUIM_NList_Display.

        This form overrides DisplayHook during construction. Later setting
        either attribute replaces the common hook pointer and selects that
        form. Output strings remain borrowed, and the internal caller
        ignores the return value.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook, MUIM_NList_Display

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DisplayRecall

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DisplayRecall -- Invalidate cached display pointers.

    INPUTS
        Set only; BOOL conventionally TRUE. The implementation ignores the
        supplied value.

    FUNCTION
        Clears the cached payload pointer so the next display-array lookup
        invokes the display callback again when a payload or title is
        available. FALSE has the same invalidating effect as TRUE. This
        setter does not call the hook immediately, redraw the list, or
        invalidate every geometry cache.

        Use it before NList reuses buffers changed outside a display
        callback. Request MUIM_NList_Redraw for affected entries when
        changed text requires measurement or repainting. It does not
        transfer or extend buffer ownership.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook, MUIM_NList_Redraw

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DoubleClick

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DoubleClick -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        You'll get a notify on it each time the user double
        clicks on an entry in the list, or on the title.

        The value is the entry number, which will be -1 when
        it's the title.

        You'll get a notify on it too when you press the 'return' key
        and the NList object is active or default.

        If you get() it, you'll get the last click position, which
        can be -2 if it was on nothing (ie not entry or title).

        For triple clicks and more, use MUIA_NList_MultiClick.

    NOTE
        Disabled for the title when MUIA_NList_TitleClick is used.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_MultiClick, MUIA_NList_TitleClick,
        MUIA_NList_EntryClick, MUIM_NList_TestPos.
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DragColOnly

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DragColOnly -- [ISG], LONG

    FUNCTION
        When set to a col number (displayhook col number, not the visible
        one), only the text of that col for the selected entry will
        be dragged instead of the visible part of the entry.

        Set it to -1 to come back to standard/default mode when you have
        changed it !
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DragSortable

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DragSortable -- [ISG], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIA_List_DragSortable.

        DragType will be MUIV_NList_DragType_Default unless you set it.

        No need for you to set MUIA_Dropable or MUIA_Draggable.

    DEFAULT
        FALSE
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DragSortInsert

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DragSortInsert -- [..GN], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        Same as MUIA_NList_InsertPosition but the notify is made only after
        a MUIA_NList_DragSortable move.
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DragType

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DragType -- [ISG], LONG

    SPECIAL INPUTS
        MUIV_NList_DragType_None        no drag
        MUIV_NList_DragType_Default     as set in prefs.
        MUIV_NList_DragType_Immediate   drag on borders and with qualifier, and
                                        immediate drag if non-multiselect mode.
        MUIV_NList_DragType_Borders     drag on borders and with qualifier.
        MUIV_NList_DragType_Qualifier   drag only using qualifier.

    FUNCTION
        Same function as Listview.mui/MUIA_Listview_DragType.

        If you want the user to be able to drag items out of
        your list, you must set this.
        Don't use MUIA_Draggable with NList or NListview.
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DropMark

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DropMark -- [..G], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIA_List_DropMark.

        After a successful drop operation, this attribute holds
        the position where we should insert the new entry(ies).

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DropType, MUIM_NList_DropType, MUIM_NList_DropDraw
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_DropType

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_DropType -- [..G], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Same function as MUIA_NList_DropMark but will return the
        current DropMark type instead of the DropMark entry number.

        After a successful drop operation, this attribute holds
        the type of dropmark which where drawn.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DropMark, MUIM_NList_DropType, MUIM_NList_DropDraw
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Entries

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Entries -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIA_List_Entries.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_First, MUIA_NList_Visible, MUIA_NList_Active
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_EntryClick

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_EntryClick -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        You'll get a notify on it each time the user click
        on an entry in the list (on the title, use
        MUIA_NList_TitleClick for that).

        The value is the entry number.

        You'll get a notify on it too when you press the 'return' key
        and the NList object is active or default, but only if there
        is no notify asked on MUIA_NList_DoubleClick.

        If you get() it, you'll get the last click position, which
        can be -1 when on title and -2 if it was on nothing.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_MultiClick, MUIA_NList_TitleClick,
        MUIA_NList_DoubleClick, MUIM_NList_TestPos.
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_EntryValueDependent

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_EntryValueDependent -- [ISG], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        If your display hook return different strings when the
        entry num value change for a identical entry pointer
        then you should set it.

    DEFAULT
        FALSE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Exports

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Exports -- [ISG], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Tell the NList object what parts of it's state will have to
        be saved in MUIM_Export method (which is called by the
        MUIM_Application_Save method if the object has a ObjectID).

    SPECIAL INPUTS
        MUIV_NList_Exports_Active       save active entry number.
        MUIV_NList_Exports_Selected     save selected entries numbers.
        MUIV_NList_Exports_First        save first visible entry number.
        MUIV_NList_Exports_ColWidth     save widths of columns.
        MUIV_NList_Exports_ColOrder     save order of columns.
        MUIV_NList_Exports_Cols         save all about columns (width and order actually).
        MUIV_NList_Exports_All

    NOTE
        MUIV_NList_Exports_Selected can make a very long export (so a big program
        .cfg file) if the list has many selected entries. It take one long int
        (ie 4 bytes) for each selected entry...

        Active and First are always exported by the MUIM_Export method,
        having MUIV_NList_Exports_Active and MUIV_NList_Exports_First set or not.

    DEFAULT
        (MUIV_NList_Exports_Active | MUIV_NList_Exports_First | MUIV_NList_Exports_Cols)

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Imports
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_First

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_First -- Read or set the first visible display row.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get/notify; LONG, initial value zero. MUIA_List_First
        is an alias.

    VALUES
        Zero-based display index or MUIV_NList_First_Top (-2), Bottom (-3),
        Up (-4), Down (-5), PageUp (-6), PageDown (-7), Up2 (-8), Down2
        (-9), Up4 (-10), Down4 (-11).

    FUNCTION
        Scroll without changing Active. Bottom uses Entries minus Visible,
        bounded at zero. Page steps use Visible minus one; Up/Down variants
        step one, two or four rows. An explicit in-range index is accepted
        without clamping to the last full page. Empty lists, invalid indices
        and unchanged values do not scroll.

        Constructor First is applied only in a source-data initialization
        branch. Get returns the stored index even when the object is hidden;
        it is not a visibility test. Set may rewrite the tag to the resolved
        index or TAG_IGNORE. Use completed layout for meaningful page sizes.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Visible, MUIM_NList_Jump

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_ForcePen

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_ForcePen -- [ISG], LONG

    SPECIAL INPUTS
        MUIV_NList_ForcePen_On
        MUIV_NList_ForcePen_Off
        MUIV_NList_ForcePen_Default

    FUNCTION
        Set the ForcePen mode, when on it force the 'selected pen' color
        in all the selected area. Else the color is forced only at the
        beginning of the area and can be changed by text escape sequences.

        The default is set by the user in the pref .mcp class.

        Getting in will give its current value.
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Format

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Format -- Define the columns and their layout.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get; borrowed NUL-terminated STRPTR. Omission creates
        one default column. MUIA_List_Format is a compatibility form with a
        different one-column width default.

    FUNCTION
        Separate column descriptions with commas. Empty descriptions are
        allowed. NList counts commas before parsing; there is no quoted-
        comma escape at this stage. The count must be less than
        DISPLAY_ARRAY_MAX (64), so this implementation accepts at most 63
        columns. Use distinct COL indices from 0 through 63. A visible
        column is a position in the list; a COL index selects a string in
        the display callback array. Reordering changes the former, not the
        latter.

        The class retains the Format pointer for get; it does not copy or
        free that string. Keep it valid while installed. Parsed column
        descriptors and PREPARSE strings are separate pooled copies. Setting
        Format reparses it even when unchanged and resets the column order
        and user-width overrides. Set a new format before inserting entries
        to avoid repeated measurement and wrapping.

        At construction, explicit NULL fails; omitting the tag uses an empty
        format. Setting NULL later parses an empty format and stores NULL
        for get. Construction fails when the parser reports allocation/count
        failure. The setter ignores that result: get can return the newly
        supplied pointer while the old layout remains. A per-column ReadArgs
        failure does not reject the whole format; defaults can remain.
        Preparse allocation failure can omit that preparse. Use a valid
        format; this API is not a format-validation service.

    TEMPLATE
        DELTA=D/N,PREPARSE=P/K,COL=C/N,BAR/S,TBAR/S,NOBAR=NB/S,SIMPLEBAR=SBA
        R/S,NOTITLEBUTTON=NOTB/S,WEIGHT=W/N,MINWIDTH=MIW/N,MAXWIDTH=MAW/N,CO
        LWIDTH=CW/N,MINCOLWIDTH=MICW/N,MAXCOLWIDTH=MACW/N,PIXWIDTH=PW/N,MINP
        IXWIDTH=MIPW/N,MAXPIXWIDTH=MAPW/N,PARTCOLSUBST=PCS/K

    OPTIONS
        DELTA (D): Gap in pixels after a column, default 4; ignored after
        the last visible column. Negative values become zero. A bar raises a
        gap below 2 by 2.

        PREPARSE (P): Text parsed before each column string and its entry-
        specific preparse. COL (C): Display-array index, default the
        original column position. Duplicate indices are reassigned by the
        parser; do not rely on the resulting order. Out-of-range indices are
        not validated as safe display-array indices.

        TBAR: Separator in the title only, the default. BAR: Separator
        through the column. NOBAR (NB): No separator; overrides BAR and
        TBAR. SIMPLEBAR (SBAR): Adds the simple-line flag. NOTITLEBUTTON
        (NOTB): Disables title-button behavior for this descriptor.

        WEIGHT (W): Relative share of the space left after fixed/content
        widths and gaps. Positive values are weights, not unconditional
        percentages of total list width. Negative values select content
        measurement. The last original column defaults to 100; WEIGHT=-1
        there is also converted to 100, while -2 requests content width.
        Other columns default to content width. The legacy List_Format one-
        column case keeps content width.

        COLWIDTH (CW): Fixed width in font increments, at least 1. PIXWIDTH
        (PW): Fixed width in pixels, at least 4. PIXWIDTH takes precedence
        over COLWIDTH, which takes precedence over WEIGHT, regardless of
        their order in the string.

        MINWIDTH/MAXWIDTH (MIW/MAW): Percentage limits.
        MINCOLWIDTH/MAXCOLWIDTH (MICW/MACW): Limits in font increments, with
        one pixel added by layout. MINPIXWIDTH/MAXPIXWIDTH (MIPW/MAPW):
        Pixel limits. These limits apply to positive weighted widths; user
        overrides, fixed widths and content-width columns bypass them.
        Layout takes the greatest minimum and smallest maximum, applies a
        maximum only when it exceeds 10 pixels, then enforces the minimum
        and at least 6 pixels.

        For parsed descriptions, MINWIDTH defaults to 5; values below 1
        become the content-width minimum (-1), so MINWIDTH=0 does not
        disable the minimum. MAXWIDTH defaults to 1000 after normalization,
        not 10000. MINCOLWIDTH defaults to 0 and MAXCOLWIDTH to 5000;
        MINPIXWIDTH defaults to 6 and MAXPIXWIDTH to 20000. A final empty
        description retains initial zero maximum fields because it is not
        passed through normalization.

        PARTCOLSUBST (PCS): DISABLED, LEFT, CENTER or RIGHT. The parser
        selects by the first letter, ignoring case. When enabled in user
        preferences, the renderer can replace clipped text with an ellipsis
        at that position. The default is DISABLED.

    EXAMPLE
        Format "COL=0 PW=24,COL=1 W=100" assigns a 24-pixel first column and
        a weighted second column. Fill strings[0] and strings[1] in the
        display callback; visible reordering does not change those indices.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook, MUIA_NList_Columns, MUIM_NList_ColWidth

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Horiz_DeltaFactor

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_DeltaFactor -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Used for NListview. You can make a notification on it
        if you want to attach your own horizontal scrollbar
        and set the increment value of scrollbar's arrows :

        DoMethod(NLobj, MUIM_Notify, MUIA_NList_Horiz_DeltaFactor,MUIV_EveryTime,
          SBobj, 3, MUIM_Set,MUIA_Prop_DeltaFactor,MUIV_TriggerValue);

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_Entries, MUIA_NList_Horiz_First, MUIA_NList_Horiz_Visible
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Horiz_Entries

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_Entries -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Used for NListview. You can make a notification on it
        if you want to attach your own horizontal scrollbar :

        DoMethod(NLobj, MUIM_Notify, MUIA_NList_Horiz_Entries,MUIV_EveryTime,
          SBobj, 3, MUIM_Set,MUIA_Prop_Entries,MUIV_TriggerValue);

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_DeltaFactor, MUIA_NList_Horiz_First, MUIA_NList_Horiz_Visible
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Horiz_First

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_First -- Read or set horizontal scroll offset.

    INPUTS
        Set/get/notify; LONG pixel offset, initially zero.

    VALUES
        Pixel position or MUIV_NList_Horiz_First_Start (-2), End (-3), Left
        (-4), Right (-5), PageLeft (-6), PageRight (-7), Left2 (-8), Right2
        (-9), Left4 (-10), Right4 (-11).

    FUNCTION
        Start requests zero; End uses content width minus visible width.
        Left/Right use one font X-size step, with the numbered forms
        multiplying that step. PageLeft/PageRight use half the visible
        width. Accepted offsets are constrained against the content and
        viewport widths.

        Use valid laid-out font and width data. The implementation does not
        consistently clamp the final subtraction to zero when content is
        narrower than the viewport; do not assume every accepted value
        remains nonnegative. Set can rewrite tags and request redraw.

        For an external scrollbar, connect this attribute to Prop_First
        using NoNotifySet in both directions. NListview already provides
        this connection while its horizontal scrollbar is attached.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_Entries, MUIA_NList_Horiz_Visible

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Horiz_Visible

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_Visible -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Used for NListview. You can make a notification on it
        if you want to attach your own horizontal scrollbar :

        DoMethod(NLobj, MUIM_Notify, MUIA_NList_Horiz_Visible,MUIV_EveryTime,
          SBobj, 3, MUIM_NoNotifySet,MUIA_Prop_Visible,MUIV_TriggerValue);

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Horiz_DeltaFactor, MUIA_NList_Horiz_Entries, MUIA_NList_Horiz_First
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_IgnoreSpecialChars

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_IgnoreSpecialChars -- [ISG], const char *

    FUNCTION
        Used to let NList ignore a list of user definable characters during
        string parsing. Some greek fonts need the 0xA0 character to display
        an Alpha characters. In this case the string "\xa0" should be supplied
        to make NList ignore this character and not treat 0xA0 as a "non
        breaking space".
        This string will *NOT* be copied by NList and must remain valid as long
        as the object exists!

    DEFAULT
        NULL
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Imports

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Imports -- [ISG], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Tell the NList object what parts of it's state must be loaded
        (and used) in MUIM_Import method (which is called by the
        MUIM_Application_Load method if the object has a ObjectID).

    SPECIAL INPUTS
        MUIV_NList_Imports_Active     load previous active entry number (and make it active).
        MUIV_NList_Imports_Selected   load previous selected entries numbers (and make them selected).
        MUIV_NList_Imports_First      load previous first visible entry number (and make it the first visible).
        MUIV_NList_Imports_ColWidth   load previous widths of columns (and set them).
        MUIV_NList_Imports_ColOrder   load previous order of columns (and set it).
        MUIV_NList_Imports_Cols       load all about columns (width and order actually).
        MUIV_NList_Imports_All

    DEFAULT
        (MUIV_NList_Imports_Active | MUIV_NList_Imports_First | MUIV_NList_Imports_Cols)

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Exports
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Input

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Input -- Enable ordinary interactive entry selection.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set/get; BOOL, default TRUE. MUIA_Listview_Input is a
        compatibility alias.

    FUNCTION
        A transition to FALSE clears Active and line-selection flags,
        selects the read-list frame and requests redraw. A transition to
        TRUE selects the input-list frame and requests redraw. This does not
        alter payload ownership.

        FALSE is not a universal interaction lock: navigation has a separate
        read-only path, and character-range Select handling is chosen by
        TypeSelect. Use the selection mode appropriate to the content. Get
        returns the stored input flag.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_TypeSelect, MUIA_NList_MultiSelect

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_InsertPosition

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_InsertPosition -- [..GN], LONG

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIA_List_InsertPosition.

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_KeepActive

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_KeepActive -- [.S.], Obj *

    FUNCTION
        Usefull when MUIA_NList_DefaultObjectOnClick if you
        don't want the specified object to be desactivated.
        A NListview object set it to it's NList child at
        creation time, so you should not have to use that tag.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_DefaultObjectOnClick,MUIA_NList_MakeActive
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_KeyUpFocus

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_KeyUpFocus -- [ISG], Object *

    FUNCTION
        Allows to specify a specific object that will be set as the new
        window's active object in case the user pressed the UP cursor key
        and the NList object itself doesn't have anything further to
        scroll up.

